   "note": "Dimensions and outlines read off the side, plan and front views (tools/trace/b763-passenger/); the figures are the aircraft's measurements, not a copy of the drawing. The 767-300 and -300ER passenger aircraft as Boeing draws them: plain wingtips (the document has no winglet sheet; most US 767-300ERs now fly with blended winglets, which this shape doesn't have), and door 2 (an option on the -300 and -300ER). Checks over 1.5%: the side view draws the fuselage 5.54 m tall between its own dimension arrows, labelled 5.41 m (+2.4%, as on the 767-300F's sheet); the tube is fit to the printed height (fit_height.py). The plan draws the wing tip's leading-edge corner 34.15 m from the nose and its straight tip 1.71 m long, where it prints 32.87 m and 2.29 m; the 767-200 and 767-300F sheets draw the same tip, 3.1 m apart (the -300's forward plug) where their printed figures are 2.47 m apart, so the shape keeps the drawing's (a 2.08 m tip chord with its rounded corner). Also kept from the drawing: the engine's inlet 18.92 m from the nose (the side view; the plan draws it at 18.17 m, printed 18.72 m RR/PW, 18.54 m GE), and door 2's middle at 16.6 m (drawn there here and on the door sheet 2.7.1, whose figure is 15.96 m)."
  }
